steps.body:
A graph is a set of nodes joined by edges. Number the nodes 0 to n-1 and store an *adjacency list*: `adj[u]` holds every neighbor of `u`. An undirected edge goes both ways, so add it to both lists.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    int n = 0;
    int m = 0;
    std::cin >> n >> m;
    {{code}}
    std::cout << "degrees:";
    for (const auto& neighbors : adj) {
        std::cout << ' ' << neighbors.size();
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```stdin
7 5
0 3
1 6
3 5
5 0
6 4
```

```cpp
std::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);
for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
    int u = 0;
    int v = 0;
    std::cin >> u >> v;
    adj[u].push_back(v);
    adj[v].push_back(u);
}
```

Each inner vector owns its own heap buffer, so memory is O(n + m), far less than an n by n matrix when edges are few. The degree of `u` is `adj[u].size()`. Neighbors appear in input order, so `std::sort` each list whenever the visiting order reaches the output.

DFS works like the recursive flood fill, with `adj[u]` in place of the four grid directions. It marks a node in a `std::vector<bool> visited`, then recurses into every unvisited neighbor:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

{{code}}

int main() {
    const std::vector<std::vector<int>> adj = {{3, 5}, {6}, {}, {0, 5}, {6}, {3, 0}, {1, 4}};
    std::vector<bool> visited(adj.size(), false);
    int components = 0;
    for (int u = 0; u < static_cast<int>(adj.size()); ++u) {
        if (!visited[u]) {
            dfs(u, adj, visited);
            ++components;
        }
    }
    std::cout << "components: " << components << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
void dfs(int u, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, std::vector<bool>& visited) {
    visited[u] = true;
    for (int v : adj[u]) {
        if (!visited[v]) {
            dfs(v, adj, visited);
        }
    }
}
```

Loop over the nodes in `main` and call `dfs` on each one that is still unvisited. Each of those calls reaches exactly one *connected component*, so counting them counts the components.

> Gotcha: each nested call is a stack frame. With the 8 MB stack here, the `dfs` in the example crashes on a single path of about 80,000 nodes, so very deep graphs need an iterative DFS with your own `std::stack`.

