steps.body:
In a directed graph the edge `a b` goes one way, so only `adj[a].push_back(b)`. A *topological order* lists every node so that each edge points forward, like taking courses after their prerequisites.

Kahn's algorithm tracks each node's *in-degree*, the number of edges coming into it. A node with in-degree 0 is ready. Take a ready node, append it to the order, and remove its outgoing edges by lowering each neighbor's in-degree. A neighbor that drops to 0 becomes ready.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    int n = 0;
    int m = 0;
    std::cin >> n >> m;
    std::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(n);
    std::vector<int> indeg(n, 0);
    for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
        int a = 0;
        int b = 0;
        std::cin >> a >> b;
        adj[a].push_back(b);
        ++indeg[b];
    }
    std::vector<int> order;
    {{code}}
    for (int u : order) {
        std::cout << u << ' ';
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```stdin
6 6
5 2
5 0
4 0
4 1
2 3
3 1
```

```cpp
std::priority_queue<int, std::vector<int>, std::greater<int>> ready;
for (int u = 0; u < n; ++u) {
    if (indeg[u] == 0) {
        ready.push(u);
    }
}
while (!ready.empty()) {
    int u = ready.top();
    ready.pop();
    order.push_back(u);
    for (int v : adj[u]) {
        --indeg[v];
        if (indeg[v] == 0) {
            ready.push(v);
        }
    }
}
```

Most graphs have many valid orders. A min-heap always takes the smallest ready node, so you get the smallest valid order (the lexicographically smallest one) no matter how the edges were listed. A plain `std::queue` also gives a valid order, but which one depends on the input.

If the order ends with fewer than n nodes, the rest never reached in-degree 0. Each of them sits on a cycle or depends on a node that does, so no valid order exists.

