steps.body:
With weights, the fewest edges is not always the cheapest path. Dijkstra's algorithm finds the cheapest distance from a source to every node when no weight is negative. Store neighbors as `(neighbor, weight)` pairs and keep a min-heap of `(distance, node)` pairs so the closest known node is always on top.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    int n = 0;
    int m = 0;
    std::cin >> n >> m;
    std::vector<std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>> adj(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
        int u = 0;
        int v = 0;
        int w = 0;
        std::cin >> u >> v >> w;
        adj[u].push_back({v, w});
    }
    const long long INF = LLONG_MAX;
    const int src = 0;
    std::vector<long long> dist(n, INF);
    {{code}}
    for (int u = 0; u < n; ++u) {
        std::cout << u << ": ";
        if (dist[u] == INF) {
            std::cout << "INF";
        } else {
            std::cout << dist[u];
        }
        std::cout << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}
```

```stdin
5 6
0 1 4
0 2 1
2 1 2
1 3 1
2 3 5
4 3 7
```

```cpp
using Item = std::pair<long long, int>;
std::priority_queue<Item, std::vector<Item>, std::greater<Item>> pq;
dist[src] = 0;
pq.push({0, src});
while (!pq.empty()) {
    auto [d, u] = pq.top();  // copy, then pop
    pq.pop();
    if (d > dist[u]) {
        continue;  // stale entry
    }
    for (const auto& [v, w] : adj[u]) {
        if (d + w < dist[v]) {
            dist[v] = d + w;
            pq.push({dist[v], v});
        }
    }
}
```

*Relaxing* an edge asks whether going through `u` beats the best distance known for `v`. A heap cannot lower an entry it already holds, so an improved node is pushed again and the old entry stays behind. When a popped `d` is larger than `dist[u]`, that entry is stale: skip it.

Distances are `long long` because a sum of many weights can pass 2 billion. They start at `INF = LLONG_MAX`, and nothing is ever added to `INF`, because only popped nodes, which have real distances, relax edges. Nodes still at `INF` at the end are unreachable. Negative weights break Dijkstra, because a popped distance is assumed final.

